From: Tab5 Linux FPGA maintainers
Subject: [PATCH] spi: gpio: honor the bitbang half-period delay

spi-gpio currently ignores spi-bitbang's nsecs argument and clocks as
fast as GPIO accesses allow. That may exceed an SD card's 400 kHz
initialization limit on a fast CPU. Use a busy nanosecond delay for
each half-cycle, as the generic bitbang loops request.

spi_bitbang_setup_transfer() computes nsecs = (NSEC_PER_SEC / 2) / hz;
400000 Hz therefore gives exactly 1250 ns for each half-period. GPIO
access and preemption add time instead of intentionally omitting the
delay. This is a configured timing bound, not a measured wire speed.

This changes every spi-gpio controller in this kernel; it lowers their
throughput to respect requested rates. Tab5 currently has only the new
microSD GPIO SPI controller. No sleep, IRQ masking or DMA is introduced.

diff --git a/drivers/spi/spi-gpio.c b/drivers/spi/spi-gpio.c
--- a/drivers/spi/spi-gpio.c
+++ b/drivers/spi/spi-gpio.c
@@ -5,6 +5,7 @@
  * Copyright (C) 2006,2008 David Brownell
  * Copyright (C) 2017 Linus Walleij
  */
+#include <linux/delay.h>
 #include <linux/gpio/consumer.h>
 #include <linux/kernel.h>
 #include <linux/mod_devicetable.h>
@@ -80,12 +81,12 @@
 }
 
 /*
- * NOTE:  this clocks "as fast as we can".  It "should" be a function of the
- * requested device clock.  Software overhead means we usually have trouble
- * reaching even one Mbit/sec (except when we can inline bitops), so for now
- * we'll just assume we never need additional per-bit slowdowns.
- */
-#define spidelay(nsecs)	do {} while (0)
+ * Honor spi-bitbang's requested half-period. GPIO access adds overhead,
+ * so the effective clock can be slower than the requested frequency.
+ * In particular, MMC/SD SPI initialization must not run flat out when
+ * the device limits its clock to 400 kHz.
+ */
+#define spidelay(nsecs)	ndelay(nsecs)
 
 #include "spi-bitbang-txrx.h"
 
